:title("Python (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a large Python subset - with significant indentation - to an
LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle
runtime (llvm.RunJS).

The blocks are recognized with real INDENT/DEDENT handling: :script() rules keep an
indentation stack on the persistent parser stack, a suite is ':' NEWLINE INDENT
statements DEDENT, and every statement checks that it starts at the level of its block.
The one-line form (if c: x = 1) and semicolon separated small statements are accepted
too. Comments (#) and blank lines are skipped by the newline rule. Indentation must use
spaces (no tabs).

Statements: def (nested, closures, decorators, PEP 695 type parameters), async def
(compiled as a plain function - there is no event loop, so await is the identity on its
operand), class, if/elif/else, while and for (both with the trailing else that runs when
the loop was not broken), break/continue/pass/return, try/except/else/finally, raise,
with, assert, global/nonlocal, del, import, plain / parallel / augmented / annotated
assignment, and the walrus operator.

Functions: positional parameters, default values (evaluated once, where the def runs),
*args, **kwargs, keyword-only parameters after a '*' and the '/' positional-only marker.
Calls take positional, keyword and *seq / **map arguments; the def registers its
parameter shape (js_pysig) and the call site's keyword dict is bound to it at run time
(pyBindCall).

Classes: single AND multiple inheritance, with the MRO C3-linearized once when the class
object is built, so attribute reads and method calls stay a flat walk. The class body is
a suite that runs in a scope of its own, and everything it binds becomes a class member
(methods, class attributes, __slots__). Instance and class attribute reads follow
Python's order and run a __get__ / __set__ descriptor found on the class; __slots__
rejects an undeclared attribute. isinstance(), type() and the operator dunders (__add__,
__eq__, __lt__, __getitem__, __setitem__, __contains__, __len__, __call__, __neg__,
__matmul__ and the reflected forms) are all dispatched. The builtin exception types are
ordinary classes, so 'class MyError(Exception)' is nothing special.

Generators are REAL: a def whose body contains a yield becomes a generator function
(js_genfn), its body runs on its own goroutine and yield is a suspending handshake -
never a replay. yield from, send() and StopIteration carrying the return value all work.
Generator expressions parse (parenthesized and as the sole argument of a call) but are
materialized eagerly.

Values and expressions: int and float literals, single, double and triple quoted strings
with escapes, f-strings (f'x={a + 1}' renders like str()), True/False/None, the Ellipsis
'...', lists with negative indexing and slices (s[1:3], s[:2], s[-3:], s[::-1]), tuples
(this subset has ONE sequence type - a tuple is a list that is not written to), dicts
with insertion order, list and dict comprehensions (the variable does not leak),
comparison chaining evaluating each operand once, and/or/not with Python truthiness
(empty containers are falsy) and operand values, in / not in, is / is not, + - * / // %
with Python semantics (7/2 == 3.5, -7//2 == -4, -7%2 == 1), unary - and ~, the **
power operator (right associative, and reaching __pow__ / __rpow__ on a user class)
and the @ matrix operator (which only ever reaches __matmul__).

Numbers: decimal, hexadecimal, octal and binary integer literals, floats with an
exponent and a leading or trailing dot, '_' digit separators in all of them, and
imaginary literals - complex arithmetic (+ - * /, equality, .real / .imag) is real.
An integer literal too large for a double becomes an ARBITRARY PRECISION integer
(js_bigint, backed by Go's math/big), so 10**28-sized arithmetic is exact here; the
interpreter grammar, which has only doubles, cannot do that - the one place where
the compiled path reaches further than the interpreted one.

Exceptions: every except clause of a try statement is kept and its TYPE decides,
through isinstance, so a tuple of classes and a user hierarchy both work; an untyped
'except:' catches everything and an unmatched value is re-raised (after this
statement's finally ran). A bare 'raise' re-raises the active exception, 'raise X
from e' records e as X.__cause__, and 'except* T as g' (PEP 654) runs every clause
whose type matches part of an ExceptionGroup, each with its own sub-group.

Type syntax: annotations are recorded in the enclosing body's __annotations__, PEP
695 'type NAME = TYPE' binds an alias object with __name__ and __value__, and
list[int] is a generic alias value (one object per written alias, so two of them
compare equal).

Sets are a real value type: the literal and the comprehension deduplicate with
Python's ==, and the algebra (union, intersection, difference, symmetric
difference, the subset orderings), membership, len, iteration and rendering all
work. { } alone stays the empty DICT, as in Python. The | ^ & << >> operators are
the set algebra on two sets and the 32 bit integer operators on numbers.

Slices carry a STEP: s[lo:hi:step] reading (s[::-1] is the reversal), as an
assignment target (a step of 1 splices and may change the length, an extended step
assigns element by element) and as a del target. A right hand side may be a bare
tuple, so 'one = 7,' is the one-element tuple.

String literals are a run of ADJACENT pieces, which Python concatenates implicitly,
each with its own prefix and quote style: r keeps the backslashes, b makes a bytes
value (a list of byte numbers, so indexing, + and len are the ordinary list
machinery), u is accepted and ignored, and f makes the piece an f-string. The
escapes are Python's - the single letter ones, the three digit octal form, the two
digit hex form, the four and the eight digit Unicode forms, the named character
form (from a small name table) and a backslash before a newline, which joins the
lines. len() counts CODE POINTS, so an astral character is one.

f-strings carry the whole 3.12 surface: all four quote styles, the rf / fr prefix
orders, a nested f-string inside a replacement field, the !r / !s / !a conversions,
the = debug form that echoes the source text of its expression, and the format mini
language ([[fill]align][sign][0][width][.precision][type], with a {nested} field
allowed inside the spec) rendered by js_pyformat.

Assignment takes a full TARGET LIST: a, *b = xs and (h, (i, j)) = t unpack (the
targets after the starred one are read from the end, so the rest needs no length
arithmetic), a chain a = b = value writes one value to every head, and the same
writer binds the loop target, so 'for head, *tail in xs' works. A display may
spread: [0, *xs], (0, *xs), {0, *s} and {**d, 'k': v}.

The match statement (PEP 634) is genuinely lowered to control flow: literal and
value patterns, or-patterns, capture / wildcard / as patterns, sequence patterns
with a starred rest, mapping patterns with **rest, class patterns (positional
through __match_args__, keyword through attributes, and the builtin type names) and
case guards. A capture binds an ordinary variable.

Still missing: nothing the full-syntax ratchet (tests/python-test-full.py) covers -
it parses and passes in full.

Functions become IR functions jsf_N(env, args); Python scoping goes through the
js_pyset_var external: a def, a lambda, a class body and the module top level are
BINDING BOUNDARIES (js_pyfnscope), so an assignment binds locally even when an
enclosing function binds the same name, while global / nonlocal send it to the
module scope or the enclosing binding. A try, except, with or finally body is its
own IR closure but NOT a boundary, so what it assigns belongs to the function
around it. Negative
indexing and dict entries through js_pyget/js_pyset, dicts through js_dict_new (two
parallel key/value arrays keep the insertion order), slices through js_pyslice,
iteration through js_pyiter, len through js_pylen, floor division and modulo through
js_fdiv/js_fmod, truthiness through js_pytruthy, every user visible operator through the
js_pybin dispatcher (which gives a user class the chance to answer it with a dunder),
classes through js_pyclass_new/js_pyclass_fill/js_pygetattr/js_pysetattr/js_pymcall/
js_pycall, exceptions through js_throw/js_try, and print/f-strings through
js_pyprint/js_pystr.
The whole file becomes jsmain(env, args) and simply runs top to bottom; a top-level
main() is then entered and its return value becomes the exit status.
The same language is interpreted directly by python-interpreter.abnf, which does not
reach quite as far - it has no goroutine to suspend, so its generators cannot be the
real thing.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// The leading NL swallows any comment/blank lines at the top of the file.
Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = [ NL ] { TopStmt } ;
// Imports are module level (column 0) and yield no statement, so they are tried
// at the TopStmt level (before Statement, outside the stmtPos wrapper) and push
// nothing net - keeping the value stack balanced for buildMain(takeAll()).
TopStmt     = SameLevel ( Import | FromImport | Statement ) ;

// Module level imports. resolveImport (lib/compile-core.js) checks each module
// path against core.stdlibImports; an unresolvable one aborts (or, under
// -warn-imports, warns and continues). An import produces no statement thunk:
// DottedName pushes the bare path and the resolving tag pops it (net zero).
Import      = KwImport ImportMod { "," ImportMod } NL ;
ImportMod   <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = DottedName [ KwAs Id ] ;
FromImport  <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = KwFrom DottedName KwImport ( "*" | ImportName { "," ImportName } ) NL ;
ImportName  = Id [ KwAs Id ] ;
DottedName  <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~>
            = Id { "." Id } ;
KwImport    = "import" KwEnd ;
KwFrom      = "from" KwEnd ;
KwAs        = "as" KwEnd ;

// Compound statements own their suites; small statements end with a newline.
Statement   <~~ push(stmtPos(pop())) ~~>
            = If | While | For | TryStmt | With | Match | Decorated | DefLike | SmallLine ;

// class / def / async def push {dname, dstmt}: the NAME is what a decorator
// chain has to rebind, so the definition carries it. Used undecorated, only the
// statement thunk survives.
DefLike     <~~ push(pop().dstmt) ~~>
            = Class | AsyncDef | Def ;

// One logical line of small statements: 'a = 1; b = 2' is one line with two of
// them (a trailing ';' is allowed), so the line pushes ONE sequence thunk.
SmallLine   <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = SmallStmt { ";" SmallStmt } [ ";" ] NL ;
SmallStmt   = Return | Break | Continue | Pass | Raise | Assert | Global | Nonlocal | Del
            | TypeAlias | AugAssign | AnnAssign | Assign | ExprSmall ;

// PEP 695 'type NAME[params] = TYPE': the alias is a real value, an object whose
// __name__ is the alias name and whose __value__ is the type it stands for. It is
// tried before the expression statement, and 'type' stays the builtin function
// everywhere else - only 'type' followed by a NAME is the alias statement.
TypeAlias   <~~ push(makeTypeAlias(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd Id <~~ push({tname: up.in}) ~~> [ TypeParams ] "=" Expression ;

// A suite: ':' NEWLINE INDENT statements DEDENT. Every further statement of the
// suite starts at the same level; the first one defined the level via Indent.
// Python also allows the inline form 'if c: x = 1' - a ':' followed by a single
// line of small statements; it is tried second (a real block has a NEWLINE right
// after the ':', so the indented alternative fails fast on the inline form).
Suite       <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ":" ( NL Indent Statement { SameLevel Statement } Dedent | SmallLine ) ;

// match SUBJECT: case PATTERN [if GUARD]: SUITE ... - the structural pattern match
// of PEP 634. `match` and `case` are soft keywords, so a Match only commits once the
// `:` NEWLINE INDENT `case` shape is there; anywhere else `match` stays an ordinary
// name. Every case is tried in source order, and the first whose pattern (and guard)
// holds runs its suite. A capture writes an ordinary variable, so the bindings a
// pattern made are still there after the statement, as in Python.
Match       <~~ push(makeMatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "match" KwEnd ExprList ":" NL Indent CaseClause { SameLevel CaseClause } Dedent ;
CaseClause  <~~ push(makeCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd Patterns [ CaseGuard ] Suite ;
CaseGuard   <~~ push({guard: pop()}) ~~>
            = "if" KwEnd Expression ;
// A comma-separated pattern list is a sequence pattern (case 1, 2:); a single
// pattern with no comma stays itself.
Patterns    <~~ push(makeSeqPattern(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = OrPattern { "," <~~ push({pcomma: true}) ~~> OrPattern } [ "," <~~ push({pcomma: true}) ~~> ] ;
OrPattern   <~~ push(makeOrPattern(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ClosedPattern { "|" ClosedPattern } [ AsPattern ] ;
AsPattern   = KwAs Id <~~ push({asname: up.in}) ~~> ;
ClosedPattern = GroupPattern | SeqPatternB | MapPattern | ClassPattern | ValuePattern | LitPattern | StarPattern | WildPattern | CapturePattern ;
GroupPattern <~~ push(makeSeqPattern(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ OrPattern { "," <~~ push({pcomma: true}) ~~> [ OrPattern ] } ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SeqPatternB <~~ push(makeSeqPattern(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = "[" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ OrPattern { "," OrPattern } [ "," ] ] "]" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
MapPattern  <~~ push(makeMapPattern(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ MapItem { "," MapItem } [ "," ] ] "}" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
MapItem     = MapRest | MapEntry ;
MapRest     <~~ push({maprest: up.in}) ~~>
            = "**" Id ;
MapEntry    <~~ var p2 = pop(); push({mkey: pop(), mpat: p2}) ~~>
            = Expression ":" OrPattern ;
ClassPattern <~~ push(makeClassPattern(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DottedRef "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ ClassPatArg { "," ClassPatArg } [ "," ] ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ClassPatArg = ClassPatKw | ClassPatPos ;
ClassPatKw  <~~ var p3 = pop(); push({ckey: pop(), cpat: p3}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "=" NotEqSign OrPattern ;
ClassPatPos <~~ push({cpos: pop()}) ~~>
            = OrPattern ;
// A dotted name in a pattern is a VALUE pattern (case K.LIMIT:), a bare name a
// capture; both need the class-pattern form to be tried first.
ValuePattern <~~ push(makeValuePattern(pop())) ~~>
            = DottedRef2 ;
DottedRef   <~~ push({dname: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Id { "." Id } ;
DottedRef2  <~~ push(makeDotted(stripWs(up.in))) ~~>
            = Id "." Id { "." Id } ;
LitPattern  <~~ push(makeValuePattern(pop())) ~~>
            = ( StringLit | NegNumLit | PyNum | True | False | None ) ;
NegNumLit   <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" PyNum ;
StarPattern <~~ push({pstar: up.in}) ~~>
            = "*" Id ;
WildPattern <~~ push({wild: true}) ~~>
            = "_" KwEnd ;
CapturePattern <~~ push({capture: up.in}) ~~>
            = Id ;

If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "if" KwEnd Expression Suite { Elif } [ Else ] ;
Elif        <~~ push(makeElif(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = SameLevel "elif" KwEnd Expression Suite ;
Else        <~~ push(makeElse(pop())) ~~>
            = SameLevel "else" KwEnd Suite ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhile(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "while" KwEnd Expression Suite [ Else ] ;

// for TARGETS in ITER: the loop target is a full target list, so 'for a, b in ...',
// 'for (a, b) in ...' and 'for head, *tail in ...' all unpack each element through
// the same writer as an assignment.
// A trailing 'else' on a loop runs when the loop ended WITHOUT a break, so it
// hangs off the natural exit while break jumps past it.
For         <~~ push(makeFor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ "async" KwEnd ] "for" KwEnd TargetList "in" KwEnd Expression Suite [ Else ] ;

Def         <~~ push(makeDef(pop(), pop(), pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "def" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ParamList [ "->" DiscardType ] Suite ;
// PEP 695 type parameters - def f[T](...) / class C[A, B] - parsed and ignored.
TypeParams  = "[" TypeParam { "," TypeParam } [ "," ] "]" ;
TypeParam   = [ "**" | "*" ] Id [ ":" DiscardType ] [ "=" DiscardType ] ;
// Parameter lists: plain names, default values (evaluated once, when the def
// statement runs - like real Python), a *args rest parameter, **kwargs, the bare
// '*' and the '/' positional-only marker. Annotations parse and are ignored.
// '/' has no runtime effect here (it only FORBIDS the keyword spelling, which no
// correct program uses); everything after a '*' or '*args' is keyword-only.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParamList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ Param { "," Param } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
Param       = DblStarParam | StarParam | SlashParam | NamedParam ;
DblStarParam <~~ push(makeKwParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "**" [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" DiscardType ] ] ;
StarParam   <~~ push(makeRestParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "*" [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" DiscardType ] ] ;
SlashParam  <~~ push({pslash: true}) ~~>
            = "/" ;
NamedParam  <~~ push(makeNamedParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" DiscardType ] [ ParamDefault ] ;
ParamDefault <~~ push({pdefault: pop()}) ~~>
            = "=" Expression ;

// A decorated def / async-def / class: every decorator expression is evaluated
// where the definition runs and applied to the defined value bottom-up, then the
// result is re-bound to the definition's name - real PEP 318 / PEP 614
// semantics, so '@times(5)' and '@registry["t"]' work like any other call.
Decorated   <~~ push(makeDecorated(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Decorator { SameLevel Decorator } SameLevel ( Class | AsyncDef | Def ) ;
Decorator   <~~ push({deco: pop()}) ~~>
            = "@" Expression NL ;

// class Name[(bases, metaclass=..., ...)]: SUITE. The body is a suite that runs
// in a scope of its own; every name it binds becomes a class member (methods,
// class attributes, __slots__ - a class body really is a statement sequence, so
// 'for _step in range(3): limit += 20' inside one works). The bases are ordinary
// expressions; the MRO is C3-linearized once, when the class object is built
// (js_pyclass_new), so multiple inheritance costs nothing at lookup time.
// Keyword arguments (metaclass=...) parse and are ignored.
Class       <~~ push(makeClass(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "class" KwEnd Id <~~ push({cname: up.in}) ~~> [ TypeParams ]
              [ "(" [ ClassArg { "," ClassArg } [ "," ] ] ")" ] Suite ;
ClassArg    = ClassKw | ClassBase ;
ClassKw     <~~ pop() ~~>            = Id "=" Expression ;
ClassBase   <~~ push({cbase: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;

// async def: compiled as a plain def (the body runs synchronously). Only the
// SYNTAX is in scope here - there is no event loop, so nothing awaits.
AsyncDef    <~~ push(makeDef(pop(), pop(), pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "async" KwEnd "def" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ParamList
              [ "->" DiscardType ] Suite ;

// try/except/else/finally: genuinely implemented. EVERY except clause is kept and
// its exception TYPE decides (isinstance, so a tuple of classes works); an untyped
// 'except:' catches everything. An unmatched value is re-raised, so it reaches the
// enclosing try - after this statement's finally ran.
TryStmt     <~~ push(makeTry(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "try" KwEnd TryBody ( ExceptClause { ExceptClause } [ TryElse ] [ TryFinally ] | TryFinally ) ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Suite ;
// 'except* T as g' (PEP 654) parses too: every matching clause runs, each with the
// sub-ExceptionGroup of the leaves its type accepted (see emitStarHandlers).
ExceptClause <~~ push(makeExcept(takeAll())) ~~>
            = SameLevel "except" KwEnd [ "*" <~~ push({estar: true}) ~~> ]
              [ ExcType [ KwAs Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ] Suite ;
ExcType     <~~ push({etype: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
TryElse     <~~ push({elsebody: pop()}) ~~>
            = SameLevel "else" KwEnd Suite ;
TryFinally  <~~ push({finalbody: pop()}) ~~>
            = SameLevel "finally" KwEnd Suite ;

// raise EXPR [from CAUSE]: genuinely implemented (js_throw, recovered by the
// enclosing js_try). A bare 'raise' inside an except body re-raises the active
// exception; 'from CAUSE' stores the cause as the raised value's __cause__.
Raise       <~~ push(makeRaise(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "raise" KwEnd [ Expression [ RaiseFrom ] ] ;
RaiseFrom   <~~ push({rcause: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwFrom Expression ;

// with EXPR [as TARGET]: the real context-manager protocol - __enter__ before the
// body, __exit__ after it (and on the exception path, where a truthy answer
// swallows the exception). A context expression that defines neither binds its own
// value and just runs the body.
With        <~~ push(makeWith(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = [ "async" KwEnd ] "with" KwEnd
              ( "(" WithItem { "," WithItem } [ "," ] ")" | WithItem { "," WithItem } ) WithBody ;
WithItem    <~~ push(makeWithItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Expression [ KwAs Target ] ;
WithBody    <~~ push({withbody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Suite ;

// assert COND [, MSG]: genuinely implemented - a false condition aborts the run.
Assert      <~~ push(makeAssert(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "assert" KwEnd Expression [ "," Expression ] ;

// global / nonlocal: the declaration guarantees a binding in the right scope,
// then plain assignment (js_pyset_var: update where the chain resolves the
// name) lands there. Deviation: a nearer binding of the same name still wins
// over the declared scope (real Python forces it); nonlocal is checked when
// the call reaches it, not at compile time. del stays unsupported.
Global      <~~ push(makeGlobal(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "global" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } ;
Nonlocal    <~~ push(makeNonlocal(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "nonlocal" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } ;
// del NAME / del obj[k] / del obj.attr. A deleted local keeps its slot, holding
// a sentinel, so a later read raises UnboundLocalError (a catchable exception)
// instead of silently resolving to an enclosing binding of the same name.
Del         <~~ push(makeDel(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "del" KwEnd DelTarget { "," DelTarget } ;
DelTarget   = "(" Target { "," Target } [ "," ] ")" | Target ;


// name: Type [= value]: the annotation is parsed and ignored; with a value it is a
// plain assignment, without one it is a no-op declaration.
AnnAssign   <~~ push(makeAnnAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ":" AnnType [ "=" Expression ] ;
// The annotation of an annotated assignment, kept as its source text: it is what
// the enclosing class or module body records in __annotations__ (js_pyannot).
AnnType     <~~ push({atype: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = DiscardType ;

// A type annotation, parsed and ignored: dotted names, [..]-subscripts (commas
// allowed), | unions, the '...' of tuple[int, ...] and the STRING form
// (y: "str"), which Python allows for a forward reference.
DiscardType = TypeAtom { "|" TypeAtom } ;
TypeStr     <~~ pop() ~~> = StringLit ;
TypeAtom    = TypeStr | "..." | Id { "." Id } [ TypeSub ] | TypeSub ;
TypeSub     = "[" [ TypeItem { "," TypeItem } ] "]" ;
TypeItem    = DiscardType ;
// An expression whose value is dropped (decorator calls, except types, defaults).
DiscardExpr <~~ pop() ~~>
            = Expression ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "return" KwEnd [ Expression ] ;
Break       <~~ push(makeBreak()) ~~>    = "break" KwEnd ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinue()) ~~> = "continue" KwEnd ;
Pass        <~~ push(function(b) { return b }) ~~>  = "pass" KwEnd ;

AugAssign   <~~ push(makeAugAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "+=" | "-=" | "**=" | "*=" | "//=" | "/=" | "%=" | "@="
                     | "|=" | "&=" | "^=" | "<<=" | ">>=" ) <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> ExprList ;
// a = b = value: every head is a target LIST, so 'a, *b = xs', '(h, (i, j)) = t'
// and 'xs[1:3] = ys' all go through the same unpacking writer, and a chain of
// heads writes the one value to each of them, left to right.
// The right hand side is an expression LIST, which is the bare tuple Python allows
// without parentheses: 'one = 7,' is the one-element tuple and 'p = 1, 2' the pair.
Assign      <~~ push(makeAssignStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AssignHead { AssignHead } ExprList ;
AssignHead  <~~ push({tlist: pop()}) ~~>
            = TargetList "=" NotEqSign ;
// A comma-separated target list unpacks; a single target with no comma stays itself.
TargetList  <~~ push(makeTargetList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TargetItem { "," TargetItem } [ "," ] ;
TargetItem  = StarTarget | TupleTarget | Target ;
// *rest in a target list swallows the middle of the sequence.
StarTarget  <~~ push({startgt: pop()}) ~~>
            = "*" TargetItem ;
// A parenthesized or bracketed target list nests: (h, (i, j)) = (1, (2, 3)).
TupleTarget <~~ push(makeTargetList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ TargetItem { "," TargetItem } [ "," ] ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace)
            | "[" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ TargetItem { "," TargetItem } [ "," ] ] "]" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A comma-separated value list is a tuple; a single value with no comma stays
// itself, but a trailing comma ('one = 7,') still makes the one-element tuple.
ExprList    <~~ push(makeExprList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Expression { "," Expression } [ "," <~~ push({tcomma: true}) ~~> ] ;
// A zero-width guard that fails when the very next character is '=', so 'a == b'
// is never read as an assignment head.
NotEqSign   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        return (c.peek(0) === 61) ? abnf.newToken("\x01eq-follows", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
Target      <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { TSlice | TIndex | TAttr } ;
TIndex      <~~ push({idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;
// xs[lo:hi:step] as an assignment or del target. Tried before TIndex (the missing
// ':' makes it fail fast there), like the reading SliceSfx.
TSlice      <~~ push(makeTSlice(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ SliceLo ] ":" [ SliceHi ] [ ":" [ SliceStep ] ] "]" ;
// obj.attr = ... : an attribute target (self.x = ... in a method). The write goes
// through js_pysetattr, which honours a __set__ descriptor on the class and the
// __slots__ declaration.
TAttr       <~~ push({tattr: true, name: pop().taname, pos: up.pos}) ~~>
            = "." Id <~~ push({taname: up.in}) ~~> ;

ExprSmall   <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression ;

// Every expression production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value.
// A lambda binds loosest; then the a-if-c-else-b conditional expression; then
// the boolean/arith layers.
Expression  = Lambda | YieldExpr | Walrus | Ternary ;
// yield / yield from: an EXPRESSION in Python ('got = yield 1' hands the value
// of the next send() to got), so it lives here and 'yield 1' on a line of its
// own is that expression used as a statement. A def whose body contains one is
// compiled to a generator function (js_genfn); the body then runs on its own
// goroutine and js_yield is the suspending handshake, so these are REAL
// generators - the body is suspended and resumed, never replayed.
YieldExpr   <~~ push(makeYield(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "yield" KwEnd [ KwFrom <~~ push({yfrom: true}) ~~> ] [ Expression ] ;
// The walrus operator, name := value: it assigns AND yields the value, so it can
// introduce a binding inside a condition or a comprehension filter. Tried before
// Ternary, whose OrExpr would otherwise consume the name and then choke on the ':='.
Walrus      <~~ var wv = pop(); push(makeWalrus(pop(), wv)) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ":=" Expression ;
Ternary     <~~ push(makeTernary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = OrExpr [ "if" KwEnd OrExpr "else" KwEnd Expression ] ;
Lambda      <~~ push(makeLambda(pop(), makeParamList(takeAll()), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "lambda" KwEnd [ LamParam { "," LamParam } ] ":" Expression ;
// Lambda parameters take no annotation (the ':' ends the header); defaults,
// *args and **kwargs work exactly like in def.
LamParam    = LamDblStar | LamStar | LamNamed ;
LamDblStar  <~~ push(makeKwParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "**" [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ;
LamStar     <~~ push(makeRestParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "*" [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ;
LamNamed    <~~ push(makeNamedParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ParamDefault ] ;

OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { "or" KwEnd AndExpr } ;
AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = NotExpr { "and" KwEnd NotExpr } ;
NotExpr     = Not | Comparison ;
Not         <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "not" KwEnd NotExpr ;

// 'is' / 'is not' are identity comparisons; in this subset (== already compares
// lists and dicts by identity) they lower to the same == / != externs.
// Python CHAINS comparisons: 'a < b < c' means 'a < b and b < c', with b evaluated
// ONCE and the chain short-circuiting as soon as a link is false. That is a repetition,
// not an option, and it cannot go through foldBinary (which would compare the BOOLEAN
// result of the first link with c). makeCompare lowers the chain properly.
// 'is' / 'is not' are identity comparisons; in this subset (== already compares lists
// and dicts by identity) they lower to the same == / != externs.
Comparison  <~~ push(makeCompare(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitExpr { ( "==" | "!=" | "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> BitExpr
                    | ( "not" KwEnd "in" KwEnd ) <~~ push("not in") ~~> BitExpr
                    | ( "is" KwEnd "not" KwEnd ) <~~ push("is not") ~~> BitExpr
                    | ( "is" KwEnd ) <~~ push("is") ~~> BitExpr
                    | ( "in" KwEnd ) <~~ push("in") ~~> BitExpr } ;

// Bitwise / shift operators ( | ^ & << >> ) sit just above comparison. On two
// sets they are the set algebra, on numbers the 32 bit integer operators; js_pybin
// decides by the operand shapes. With no such operator the layer is transparent.
BitExpr     <~~ push(makeBit(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = Arith { ( "|" | "^" | "&" | "<<" | ">>" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Arith } ;

Arith       <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Term { ( "+" | "-" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Term } ;

// The floor division // must be tried before /. @ is the matrix-multiply
// operator, which has no builtin meaning: it only ever reaches __matmul__.
Term        <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Factor { ( "*" | "//" | "/" | "%" | "@" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Factor } ;

Factor      = Neg | Invert | Await | Power ;
Neg         <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" Factor ;
// ~x: the bitwise complement of the 32 bit integer value, and __invert__ on an
// instance of a user class (js_pyunary decides, like unary minus).
Invert      <~~ push(makeInvert(pop())) ~~>
            = "~" Factor ;
// await EXPR: there is no event loop here and an async def is compiled as an
// ordinary function, so awaiting is the identity on its operand - the syntax is
// recognized and the expression it suspends on is simply evaluated.
Await       = "await" KwEnd Factor ;

// a ** b exponentiation. It binds TIGHTER than unary minus on its left (-2 ** 2
// is -4) and its right operand is a Factor, so it is right associative and
// '2 ** -1' works. With no '**' the layer is transparent.
Power       <~~ push(makePower(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = PostfixExpr [ "**" Factor ] ;

// f(a), s[i], lst.append(x), obj.attr - all genuinely lowered.
PostfixExpr = CallMember ;
CallMember  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { CallSfx | MethodSfx | SliceSfx | IndexSfx | AttrSfx } ;
CallSfx     <~~ push(makeCallSfx(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ CallArg { "," CallArg } [ "," ] ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
MethodSfx   <~~ push(makeMethodSfx(popName(), takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ CallArg { "," CallArg } [ "," ] ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// obj.attr with no call: js_pygetattr - own attributes first, then the MRO of the
// class (running a __get__ descriptor found there).
AttrSfx     <~~ push({kind: "attr", name: pop().aname, pos: up.pos}) ~~>
            = "." Id <~~ push({aname: up.in}) ~~> ;
// A call argument: a plain positional expression, a keyword (name=value), or
// *seq / **map unpacking. The keyword ones are collected into a dict the callee
// side binds to its parameters (js_pysig / pyBindCall). CallKw must be tried
// before CallPos - 'a' alone is a valid expression, so the '=' is what tells
// them apart, and the PEG has to see it first.
CallArg     = CallKw | CallDblStar | CallStar | CallGenExp | CallPos ;
// f(expr for x in it): a generator expression needs no parentheses of its own
// when it is the only argument of a call.
CallGenExp  <~~ push(makeListComp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Expression [ "async" KwEnd ] "for" KwEnd Id <~~ push({cvar: up.in}) ~~>
              "in" KwEnd Expression [ CompIf ] ;
CallKw      <~~ var kv = pop(); push({ckwname: pop().kwn, ckwval: kv}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push({kwn: up.in}) ~~> "=" Expression ;
CallDblStar <~~ push({ckwspread: pop()}) ~~> = "**" Expression ;
CallStar    <~~ push({cspread: pop()}) ~~>   = "*" Expression ;
CallPos     = Expression ;
// A slice s[lo:hi] with both ends optional; tried before plain indexing (the
// missing ':' makes it fail fast there).
SliceSfx    <~~ push(makeSliceSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ SliceLo ] ":" [ SliceHi ] [ ":" [ SliceStep ] ] "]" ;
SliceLo     <~~ push({slo: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
SliceHi     <~~ push({shi: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
SliceStep   <~~ push({sstep: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
IndexSfx    <~~ push({kind:"index", idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;

// StringLit comes BEFORE the numbers and names: it is a run of adjacent pieces and
// an f-string is one of them, so an f-string is never matched on its own here (that
// would leave `f"a{1}" "b"` half-parsed - PEG has no backtracking into a taken match).
Primary     = EmptyTuple | GenExpParen | Grouped | StringLit | PyNum | EllipsisLit | True | False | None
            | ListComp | ListLit | DictComp | DictLit | SetComp | SetLit | VarRef ;

// --- Python numeric literals ---
// PEG ordered choice: the IMAGINARY forms first (they are a real literal plus a
// 'j'), then the prefixed radix forms, then float before int - the plain integer,
// which every other form starts with, has to come last or it would win by being
// tried first and leave the rest of the literal behind. Every form accepts '_'
// separators between digits (Python also allows one right after the 0x/0o/0b
// prefix), and the :whitespace() pair keeps the skip off INSIDE a literal, so
// '1 . 5' is not a float.
PyNum       = PyImagDot | PyImagNum | PyHex | PyBin | PyOct | PyFloatDot | PyFloatNum | PyInt ;

PyHex       <~~ push(makeIntLit(up.in, 16)) ~~>
            = "0" ( "x" | "X" ) :whitespace() PyHexDigs :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PyBin       <~~ push(makeIntLit(up.in, 2)) ~~>
            = "0" ( "b" | "B" ) :whitespace() PyBinDigs :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PyOct       <~~ push(makeIntLit(up.in, 8)) ~~>
            = "0" ( "o" | "O" ) :whitespace() PyOctDigs :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A named production per digit run: a :command() directly followed by a (group)
// would parse as a Times repetition in this dialect (the Times trap).
PyHexDigs   = PySeps PyHexDig PyMoreHex ;
PyMoreHex   = { PyHexDig | "_" } ;
PyHexDig    = "0"..."9" | "a"..."f" | "A"..."F" ;
PyBinDigs   = PySeps PyBinDig PyMoreBin ;
PyMoreBin   = { PyBinDig | "_" } ;
PyBinDig    = "0" | "1" ;
PyOctDigs   = PySeps PyOctDig PyMoreOct ;
PyMoreOct   = { PyOctDig | "_" } ;
PyOctDig    = "0"..."7" ;
PySeps      = { "_" } ;

// .5 / 5. / 1_0.2_5 / 1e3 / 2.5e-2: a float needs a '.' or an exponent, which is
// what keeps PyInt from being shadowed.
PyFloatDot  <~~ push(makeFloatLit(up.in)) ~~>
            = "." :whitespace() PyDigits PyExpOpt :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PyFloatNum  <~~ push(makeFloatLit(up.in)) ~~>
            = "0"..."9" :whitespace() PyFloatRest :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PyFloatRest = PyDigitsOpt ( "." PyDigitsOpt PyExpOpt | PyExp ) ;
PyExpOpt    = [ PyExp ] ;
PyExp       = ( "e" | "E" ) PySign PyDigits ;
PySign      = [ "+" | "-" ] ;
PyDigits    = "0"..."9" PyDigitsOpt ;
PyDigitsOpt = { "0"..."9" | "_" } ;

PyInt       <~~ push(makeIntLit(up.in, 10)) ~~>
            = "0"..."9" :whitespace() PyDigitsOpt :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// 1j / 2.5j: an imaginary literal. Tried first, or the real part alone would win
// and leave the 'j' behind as a stray identifier.
PyImagDot   <~~ push(makeImagLit(up.in)) ~~>
            = "." :whitespace() PyDigits PyExpOpt PyJ :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PyImagNum   <~~ push(makeImagLit(up.in)) ~~>
            = "0"..."9" :whitespace() PyDigitsOpt PyFrac PyExpOpt PyJ :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PyFrac      = [ "." PyDigitsOpt ] ;
PyJ         = "j" | "J" ;

// ( ) empty tuple and ( a, b, ... ) / ( a, ) tuples build a list value: this
// subset has one sequence type, so a tuple is an immutable list that is simply
// not written to. A single parenthesized expression ( a ) is plain grouping.
EmptyTuple  <~~ push(makeList([])) ~~>
            = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// ( expr for x in it [if c] ): a generator expression. It is materialized
// eagerly here - the sequence is the same, only the laziness is missing.
GenExpParen <~~ push(makeListComp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" Expression [ "async" KwEnd ] "for" KwEnd Id <~~ push({cvar: up.in}) ~~>
              "in" KwEnd Expression [ CompIf ] ")" ;
Grouped     <~~ push(makeParen(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) ListItem { "," <~~ push({tcomma: true}) ~~> [ ListItem ] } ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// { e for x in it [if c] } set comprehension and { a, b, ... } set literal: a real
// set value - the elements are collected into a list and js_pyset_new deduplicates
// it with Python's ==. Tried after the dict forms so { k: v } and { k: v for ... }
// still parse as dicts; { } alone stays the empty DICT, as in Python.
SetComp     <~~ push(makeSetOf(makeListComp(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = "{" Expression "for" KwEnd Id <~~ push({cvar: up.in}) ~~>
              "in" KwEnd Expression [ CompIf ] "}" ;
SetLit      <~~ push(makeSetLit(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" SetItem { "," SetItem } [ "," ] "}" ;
// A set element, or *iterable spreading its members into the set.
SetItem     = SetSpread | SetElem ;
SetSpread   <~~ push({sspread: pop()}) ~~> = "*" Expression ;
SetElem     <~~ push({selem: pop()}) ~~>   = Expression ;

// [expr for x in iterable if cond] builds a list; the variable is comprehension local.
ListComp    <~~ push(makeListComp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" Expression [ "async" KwEnd ] "for" KwEnd Id <~~ push({cvar: up.in}) ~~> "in" KwEnd Expression
              [ CompIf ] "]" ;
CompIf      <~~ push({cif: pop()}) ~~>
            = "if" KwEnd Expression ;

// {k: v for x in iterable if cond} builds a dict; tried before DictLit ('for' after
// the first entry is what tells them apart) and the variable is comprehension local.
DictComp    <~~ push(makeDictComp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" Expression ":" Expression "for" KwEnd Id <~~ push({cvar: up.in}) ~~>
              "in" KwEnd Expression [ CompIf ] "}" ;

// {k: v, ...}: keys and values are expressions; entries keep their insertion order.
// **mapping spreads another dict in, later entries overwriting earlier ones in
// place (so the merged dict keeps the FIRST insertion position of a repeated key).
DictLit     <~~ push(makeDict(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ DictItem { "," DictItem } [ "," ] ] "}" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
DictItem    = DictSplat | DictEntry ;
DictSplat   <~~ push({dsplat: pop()}) ~~>
            = "**" Expression ;
DictEntry   <~~ var dv = pop(); push({dk: pop(), dv: dv}) ~~>
            = Expression ":" Expression ;

// An f-string piece. All four quote styles, and the `rf`/`fr` prefix orders; the
// replacement fields carry the full 3.12 surface - a nested f-string, a conversion
// (!r / !s / !a), a format spec that may itself contain a {nested} field, and the
// `=` debug form that echoes the source text of the expression.
FString     <~~ push(makeFString(takeAll())) ~~>
            = FPrefix ( FTripleDq | FTripleSq | FDq | FSq ) ;
FPrefix     = ( "rf" | "rF" | "Rf" | "RF" | "fr" | "fR" | "Fr" | "FR" | "f" | "F" ) :whitespace() ;
FTripleDq   = '"""' :whitespace() { FExpr | FChunk3D } '"""' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
FTripleSq   = "'''" :whitespace() { FExpr | FChunk3S } "'''" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
FDq         = '"' :whitespace() { FExpr | FChunkD } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
FSq         = "'" :whitespace() { FExpr | FChunkS } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// up.in is the whole `{...}` field, which is what the `=` debug form needs: it echoes
// the SOURCE TEXT of the expression, whitespace included (f"{x = }" -> "x = 5").
FExpr       <~~ push(makeFField(takeAll(), up.in)) ~~>
            = "{" :whitespace(Whitespace) Expression [ FDebug ] [ FConv ] [ FSpec ] "}" :whitespace() ;
// The `=` debug form. Skipped whitespace never reaches up.in, so the spaces around
// the `=` are matched as ORDINARY TOKENS (whitespace skipping off for this
// production only) - that is what makes f"{x = }" echo "x = 5" and not "x=5".
FDebug      <~~ push({fdebug: true}) ~~>
            = :whitespace() FDbgSp "=" NotEqSign FDbgSp ;
FDbgSp      = [ @+" \t" ] ;
FConv       = "!" ( "r" | "s" | "a" ) <~~ push({fconv: up.in}) ~~> ;
FSpec       <~~ push({fspec: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = ":" :whitespace() { FSpecField | FSpecChunk } ;
FSpecField  = "{" :whitespace(Whitespace) Expression "}" :whitespace() ;
FSpecChunk  <~~ push({chunk: up.in}) ~~>
            = FSpecChar { FSpecChar } ;
FSpecChar   = "\x20"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
FChunkD     <~~ push({chunk: pyUnescape(up.in, false)}) ~~> = FCharD { FCharD } ;
FCharD      = FBrace | StrEsc2 | FPlainD ;
FChunkS     <~~ push({chunk: pyUnescape(up.in, false)}) ~~> = FCharS { FCharS } ;
FCharS      = FBrace | StrEsc2 | FPlainS ;
FChunk3D    <~~ push({chunk: pyUnescape(up.in, false)}) ~~> = FChar3D { FChar3D } ;
FChar3D     = FBrace | StrEsc2 | FPlain3D ;
FChunk3S    <~~ push({chunk: pyUnescape(up.in, false)}) ~~> = FChar3S { FChar3S } ;
FChar3S     = FBrace | StrEsc2 | FPlain3S ;
// {{ and }} are the escaped braces.
FBrace      = "{{" | "}}" ;
FPlainD     = "\x20"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e" | @"\t" | "\x7f"..."\U0010ffff" ;
FPlainS     = "\x20"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e" | @"\t" | "\x7f"..."\U0010ffff" ;
FPlain3D    = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
FPlain3S    = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;

ListLit     <~~ push(makeList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ ListItem { "," ListItem } [ "," ] ] "]" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A list element, or *iterable spreading its members into the list.
ListItem    = ListSpread | Expression ;
ListSpread  <~~ push({lspread: pop()}) ~~> = "*" Expression ;

// A string literal is a run of ADJACENT pieces - Python concatenates them implicitly
// ("ab" "cd", f"a{1}" "b") - each with its own prefix and quote style. A piece with
// an `f` prefix is an f-string (its {expressions} are parsed); every other piece is
// a constant whose PREFIX decides how it is decoded: `r` keeps the backslashes, `b`
// makes it a bytes value (a list of byte numbers), `u` is accepted and ignored.
// Triple-quoted pieces are tried before the single-quoted ones so """...""" is not
// misread as an empty "" string; their body may span newlines and contain lone or
// doubled quotes, and only three quotes in a row close it.
StringLit   <~~ push(makeStrConcat(takeAll())) ~~>
            = StrPiece { StrPiece } ;
StrPiece    = FString | TextPiece ;
TextPiece   <~~ push(makeStrPiece(up.in)) ~~>
            = [ StrPrefix ] ( TripleDqStr | TripleSqStr | DqStr2 | SqStr2 ) ;
// The prefix letters, longest first (rb before r), glued to the quote.
StrPrefix   = ( "rb" | "rB" | "Rb" | "RB" | "br" | "bR" | "Br" | "BR"
              | "r" | "R" | "b" | "B" | "u" | "U" ) :whitespace() ;
TripleDqStr = '"""' :whitespace() { TStrEsc | TdqChar | TdqQ1 | TdqQ2 } '"""' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
TripleSqStr = "'''" :whitespace() { TStrEsc | TsqChar | TsqQ1 | TsqQ2 } "'''" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A single-quoted piece. The shared DqString/SqString productions push a cooked
// value, which the prefix-aware decoder does not want, so these are spelled out.
DqStr2      = '"' :whitespace() { StrEsc2 | DqChar2 } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SqStr2      = "'" :whitespace() { StrEsc2 | SqChar2 } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
StrEsc2     = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
DqChar2     = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SqChar2     = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
TStrEsc     = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// A non-quote, non-backslash char (newlines and spaces included: the body is raw).
TdqChar     = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
TdqQ1       = '"' ( TStrEsc | TdqChar ) ;
TdqQ2       = '""' ( TStrEsc | TdqChar ) ;
TsqChar     = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
TsqQ1       = "'" ( TStrEsc | TsqChar ) ;
TsqQ2       = "''" ( TStrEsc | TsqChar ) ;
// The Ellipsis singleton: '...' is a value, and 'x is ...' has to hold.
EllipsisLit <~~ push(makeEllipsis()) ~~>       = "..." ;
True        <~~ push(makeConst(true)) ~~>      = "True" KwEnd ;
False       <~~ push(makeConst(false)) ~~>     = "False" KwEnd ;
None        <~~ push(makeConst(undefined)) ~~> = "None" KwEnd ;

VarRef      <~~ push(makeVarRef(up.in)) ~~>
            = Id ;

// A logical line break: one or more newlines (blank and comment lines collapse,
// because the whitespace skip eats spaces and # comments), or the end of the file.
NL          = RealNL | AtEOF ;
RealNL      = "\n" { "\n" } ;

AtEOF = :script(~~
    (function() {
        return (c.peek(0) === -1) ? undefined : abnf.newToken("\x01not-at-eof", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// The indentation machinery. The persistent parser stack holds the indentation
// levels; the scripts scan the spaces of the current line with c.peek().
// Indent: the line is deeper than the current level - push the new level.
Indent = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var n = 0
        while (c.peek(n) === 32) n++
        if (c.peek(n) === -1) n = -1
        var top = pop()
        if (top == null) top = 0
        push(top)
        if (n > top) { push(n); return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01expected-an-indented-block", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// Dedent: the line (or the end of file) is shallower - pop one level.
Dedent = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var n = 0
        while (c.peek(n) === 32) n++
        if (c.peek(n) === -1) n = -1
        var top = pop()
        if (top == null) top = 0
        if (n < top) { return undefined }
        push(top)
        return abnf.newToken("\x01expected-a-dedent", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// SameLevel: the line starts exactly at the current level.
SameLevel = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var n = 0
        while (c.peek(n) === 32) n++
        if (c.peek(n) === -1) n = -1
        var top = pop()
        if (top == null) top = 0
        push(top)
        return (n == top) ? undefined : abnf.newToken("\x01wrong-indentation-level", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// Whitespace never includes newlines: they are structure, not noise.
// A backslash before a newline joins the lines - Python allows that anywhere, not
// only inside brackets, so it belongs in the ordinary whitespace too.
Whitespace  = { @+" \t" | Comment | LineJoin } ;
// Inside (), [] and {} a newline is NOT structure - Python joins those lines
// implicitly - so the bracketed productions switch to this whitespace and switch
// back on the closing bracket.
WhitespaceNL = { @+" \t\r\n" | Comment | LineJoin } ;
LineJoin    = "\\" "\n" ;
Comment     = "#" :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// ---


:startScript(~~

    // Shared emitter machinery (module, handle constants, externs, loopStack
    // protocol, expression/statement builders); see the `core` knobs below.
    include("lib/compile-core.js")

    // resolveImport / notImpl (lib/compile-core.js) call fail; each compiler
    // defines it. Bare early returns are braced (MetaJS has no ASI).
    function fail(msg) { println("python compiler error: " + msg); exit(1) }

    var hidCount = 0
    // sawYield is set while the CURRENT function body is being emitted, by every
    // yield in it; emitClosure hands the answer to makeDef through genClosure, so
    // a def with a yield anywhere in it becomes a generator function.
    var sawYield = false
    var genClosure = false

    // ----- Numeric literals -----
    // numText strips the whitespace the literal rule's trailing skip picked up and
    // the '_' digit separators Python allows.
    function numText(t) { return stripWs(t).split("_").join("") }
    // An integer literal. A decimal one whose value does not fit a double EXACTLY
    // becomes a real arbitrary precision integer: js_bigint is backed by Go's
    // math/big, so 10000000000000000000000000001 - 10000000000000000000000000000
    // is 1 here. (The interpreter grammar has only doubles and cannot do this - the
    // one place where the compiled path is strictly more capable.)
    function makeIntLit(t, radix) {
        var txt = numText(t)
        if (radix != 10) {
            var digits = txt.substring(2)
            return makeConst(parseInt(digits, radix))
        }
        var v = parseInt(txt, 10)
        if (v > 9007199254740992 || v < -9007199254740992) {
            return function(b) { return {b: b, v: callExt(b, "js_bigint", [emitStr(b, txt)])} }
